        "original_id": "6a5eb914-d740-57dc-b511-e42bf5222e0b",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Places: Lower Lake Stone Jail, Sulphur Bank Mine, Eastlake, Lower Lake, Lake County, California\n- Topics: quicksilver operations, mercury production, town growth, civil order\n\nFacts:\n- From 1873 to 1883, the first quicksilver operations at the Sulphur Bank Mine drove rapid town growth and created an urgent need for civil order.\n- During that 1873 to 1883 period, the need for civil order led to the building of the Lower Lake Stone Jail.\n- The Sulphur Bank Mine covered 150 acres and became one of the most noted mercury producers in the world.\n- Eastlake in Lake County, California, was located just southwest of the Sulphur Bank Mine.\n- Eastlake was 7 miles north-northwest of Lower Lake at an elevation of 1,355 feet (413 meters).",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
